From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Fri, 12 Sep 2026 19:20:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 108/108] local: tty: serial: qcom-geni: re-setup the serial
 engine after a hibernate restore

Second half of the Bluetooth-after-resume fix. Patch 0085 restores the TLMM pad
so BT_EN drives again; this one makes uart14 work once the chip is enabled.

port->setup is a bool set once, at the end of qcom_geni_serial_port_setup(),
and cleared nowhere in the file -- not by qcom_geni_serial_shutdown(), not by
runtime PM, not by suspend or resume. Within one boot that is correct: the
serial engine keeps its configuration across an open/close cycle. Across a
cross-boot resume it is a lie, and it gates the only code that programs the
engine: the SE firmware load when the proto register does not read UART,
setup_fifos(), SE_UART_RX_STALE_CNT, geni_se_config_packing(), geni_se_init()
and geni_se_select_mode(). qcom_geni_serial_startup() sees the flag, skips all
of it, and hands back a port whose engine is at power-on values.

Textbook shape B from docs.local/METHOD.md -- "any static bool / driver flag".

Measured on x1e80100. With 0085 in, gpio116 is restored and the power sequencer
drives it high; sampling the pad every 50 ms through a power-on shows a clean
low->high at +60 ms, held ~10 s, on each of the four qca_setup() retries. So the
chip IS enabled, and it still answers nothing:

  Bluetooth: hci0: setting up wcn7850
  Bluetooth: hci0: command 0xfc00 tx timeout
  Bluetooth: hci0: Reading QCA version information failed (-110)
  Bluetooth: hci0: Retry BT power ON:0

Confirmed by repair, in two steps so the answer could not be ambiguous:

  rebind hci_uart_qca on serial0-0   -> fails identically. Fresh qca_data,
                                        fresh hci_dev, fresh IBS state machine;
                                        struct qcom_geni_serial_port untouched.
  rebind a98000.serial               -> works first try. The struct is destroyed
                                        and re-created, so port->setup is false
                                        again:
      a98000.serial: ttyHS1 at MMIO 0xa98000 ... registered
      Bluetooth: hci0: setting up wcn7850
      Bluetooth: hci0: QCA Product ID :0x00000019      <- 59 ms later
      Bluetooth: hci0: QCA setup on UART is completed
    followed by a real scan, BR/EDR and LE.

The same pair rules out the other candidate. dev_pm_opp_set_rate() in
geni_serial_set_rate() is on METHOD's list of reads that lie, and a stale rate
was the obvious suspect -- but the clk core's cached rate lives in the gcc
provider against GCC_QUPV3_WRAP1_S6_CLK, not in the consumer, so it survives an
unbind. Only the OPP table is re-created. If the rate cache were the defect the
rebind could not have fixed it, and it did.

Clearing the flag on .restore is the whole fix. An open port is re-set-up
immediately -- uart_resume_port() calls ops->startup() for a port that was
initialized -- and a closed one on its next open, which is the Bluetooth case
here since the controller is powered off before hibernating.

.restore only, so ordinary suspend/resume still skips the work: there the engine
kept its state and re-running the setup would be a pointless reprogram of a live
port. Consoles are included deliberately; their engine was reset by the same
reboot, and qcom_geni_serial_port_setup() has handled uart_console() since it
was written.

SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() is expanded by hand so .restore can differ from .resume
without relying on a designated initializer overriding the macro's.

Not board-specific: every qcom geni UART shares these pm ops.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c | 63 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 62 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -2002,6 +2002,58 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Hibernate restore: the serial engine came back from the reboot at power-on
+ * values, and port->setup says it was already programmed.
+ *
+ * port->setup is set once, at the end of qcom_geni_serial_port_setup(), and is
+ * cleared nowhere -- not by qcom_geni_serial_shutdown(), not by runtime PM, not
+ * by suspend or resume. Within one boot that is correct: the block keeps its
+ * configuration across an open/close cycle, so re-running the setup would be
+ * wasted work. Across a cross-boot resume it is a lie, and it gates the ONLY
+ * code that programs the engine -- the SE firmware load when the proto register
+ * does not read UART, setup_fifos(), SE_UART_RX_STALE_CNT,
+ * geni_se_config_packing(), geni_se_init() and geni_se_select_mode().
+ * qcom_geni_serial_startup() sees the flag, skips all of it, and hands out a
+ * port whose engine is unconfigured.
+ *
+ * Measured on x1e80100 with the WCN7850's Bluetooth half on uart14. After a
+ * cross-boot resume, powering the controller on gives:
+ *
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: setting up wcn7850
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: command 0xfc00 tx timeout
+ *   Bluetooth: hci0: Reading QCA version information failed (-110)
+ *
+ * four times over, once per qca_setup() retry. Confirmed by repair, in two
+ * steps so the answer could not be ambiguous. Rebinding only hci_uart_qca on
+ * serial0-0 -- fresh qca_data, fresh hci_dev, this struct untouched -- fails
+ * identically. Rebinding a98000.serial, which destroys and re-creates this
+ * struct, works on the first try: the version read answers in 59 ms, the
+ * firmware downloads, and the controller scans.
+ *
+ * That also rules out the other candidate. The rebind gives a fresh OPP table
+ * but does NOT clear the clk core's cached rate, which lives in the gcc
+ * provider against GCC_QUPV3_WRAP1_S6_CLK rather than in the consumer, so a
+ * stale dev_pm_opp_set_rate()/clk_set_rate() cache would have survived the
+ * rebind and the rebind would not have helped.
+ *
+ * Clearing the flag on .restore is the whole fix: an open port is re-set-up
+ * immediately, because uart_resume_port() calls ops->startup() for a port that
+ * was initialized, and a closed one is re-set-up on its next open. Only
+ * .restore, so ordinary suspend/resume -- where the engine kept its state --
+ * still skips the work. Consoles included: their engine was reset by the same
+ * reboot, and qcom_geni_serial_port_setup() has handled the console case since
+ * it was written.
+ */
+static int qcom_geni_serial_restore(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct qcom_geni_serial_port *port = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	port->setup = false;
+
+	return qcom_geni_serial_resume(dev);
+}
+
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SERIAL_QCOM_GENI_CONSOLE)
 static const struct qcom_geni_device_data qcom_geni_console_data = {
 	.console = true,
@@ -2044,10 +2096,19 @@
 	.set_rate = geni_serial_set_level,
 };
 
+/*
+ * SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS() expanded by hand so .restore can differ from .resume
+ * without relying on a designated initializer overriding the macro's.
+ */
 static const struct dev_pm_ops qcom_geni_serial_pm_ops = {
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(qcom_geni_serial_runtime_suspend,
 			   qcom_geni_serial_runtime_resume, NULL)
-	SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(qcom_geni_serial_suspend, qcom_geni_serial_resume)
+	.suspend = pm_sleep_ptr(qcom_geni_serial_suspend),
+	.resume = pm_sleep_ptr(qcom_geni_serial_resume),
+	.freeze = pm_sleep_ptr(qcom_geni_serial_suspend),
+	.thaw = pm_sleep_ptr(qcom_geni_serial_resume),
+	.poweroff = pm_sleep_ptr(qcom_geni_serial_suspend),
+	.restore = pm_sleep_ptr(qcom_geni_serial_restore),
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id qcom_geni_serial_match_table[] = {
